“I mentioned impact and glass. You supplied the car, the death, and the connection to the school.”

“Does the crash fit?”

“It fits one interpretation.”

The sour smell thickened near the drain when the supply fan changed speed. Diane heard the duct damper above the corridor move through its full arc. The red status lights on the north wall remained steady.

Evan stepped farther into the room. “If you died here or came back here, give us something we can verify.”

Diane raised her hand. “No provocation.”

“I asked for verification.”

“You addressed an unidentified listener and supplied two locations.”

Miriam faced Evan. “Let me continue without questions.”

He lowered his camera slightly.

She turned toward the west door. “I sense the anger most strongly when people watch. People expected a response, then used the response against him. He wanted one person in authority to stop it.”

The assistant looked toward Diane.

Diane kept her face still and pressed the marker.

Evan noticed the glance. “A teacher?”

Miriam shook her head. “The role feels practical. Someone with keys. Someone who enters after everyone else leaves.”

Diane’s thumb stayed on the recorder.

Evan looked at her through the doorway.

Miriam continued. “The person is female. Older than the student. She works in the building.”

No one spoke.

The supply fan ran at its new speed. Air pressed against the open fire door behind Miriam, but the closer arm held it against the stop.

Miriam’s brow tightened. “A name begins with a hard sound. T or D.”

Evan’s camera moved toward Diane.

Abby remained on Miriam.

Miriam said, “Deena.”

The assistant whispered, “Diane?”

Miriam opened her eyes.

Evan turned into the corridor. “Diane.”

Three people spoke her name within two seconds.

Diane looked at Abby. Abby’s left hand remained beneath the lens. Her right index finger touched the marker button once.

Miriam said, “Yes. Diane.”

The assistant looked at Diane as though waiting for acknowledgment.

Diane stayed outside the blue line. “Continue the reading.”

Evan lowered the camera. “She got your name.”

“Continue.”

Miriam watched her from inside the room. “You already knew I heard it upstairs.”

Evan stopped moving.

Diane said, “Abby confirmed the exposure after the classroom session. I want the current sequence preserved before we discuss it.”

Miriam nodded once. “Then I will state the limit. I heard your name before the formal start. I cannot present it as private information.”

Evan looked between them. “But she arrived at it again here.”

“After you described a dead student, a crash, the school, and a worker with keys,” Diane said. “The assistant supplied my name before Miriam confirmed it.”

The assistant’s face tightened. “I thought she was asking.”

“You answered. That belongs in the sequence.”

Miriam raised one hand toward the group. “Let her finish.”

Diane remained at the threshold. “What came before the name may still matter as testimony. It cannot become a blind identification.”

Evan pointed his camera toward Miriam. “Do you believe the presence wants Diane?”

Miriam looked at Diane rather than the lens. “I believe the impression turns toward her.”

“Why?”

“She represents the person who remains after the public leaves. The role may come from the room, from what I heard upstairs, from Diane’s position in the group, or from a presence seeking her attention.”

Diane marked each clause.

Evan said, “Ask it.”

Miriam kept her eyes on Diane. “I will describe what I perceive. I will not make Diane the object of an experiment without her consent.”

The assistant looked down at the blanket.

A faint red point appeared in the wired-glass pane of the west door.

Evan saw it first. “There.”

He crossed toward the door.

“Hold position,” Diane said.

The point remained near the lower edge of the glass. A second red point appeared beside it, then a third. They formed a short diagonal line.

Abby shifted her camera enough to include the door while keeping Miriam in frame.

Evan whispered, “Eyes.”

Diane looked past the glass. “Status lights from the panel reflection.”

“The angle changed,” Evan said.

“You changed position.”

He moved one step left. The points slid across the pane and disappeared.

Abby said, “Reflection confirmed by operator movement.”

Evan moved back. Two points returned. The third remained absent.

“The panel has three lights,” he said.

“One may fall outside the reflection angle.”

Miriam had turned away from the glass. Her attention stayed on the open fire door behind her.

“What?” the assistant asked.

Miriam lifted her right hand. “Quiet.”

The supply fan continued. Water moved through a vertical pipe beside the north panels. Somewhere beyond the west door, a relay clicked.

Miriam said, “The anger is close to the threshold.”

Diane looked at the blue tape beneath her boots. “Which threshold?”

Miriam pointed toward the open fire door behind her.

The closer arm gave a short metallic creak.

Diane stepped sideways for a clear view. The door remained against the wall stop. Evan’s camera faced Miriam, leaving the hinge side outside his frame. Abby’s wider angle included the upper half of the door and part of the closer arm. The assistant stood nearest the lower edge, approximately three feet away.

“Positions,” Diane said.

Abby began naming them.

The fire door struck the wall stop once.

The impact cracked through the room.

The assistant jumped away. Evan turned. Miriam dropped her raised hand.

The door rebounded several inches, drove back against the stop, then shuddered through the closer arm. Metal rattled above the frame. The wired glass shook hard enough to blur the red reflections.

“Back,” Diane said.

A second rattle ran through the door.

Evan moved toward it.

“Stay clear.”

The door pulled away from the stop as though the closer had released, crossed half the opening, and stopped. Its bottom edge scraped the concrete. The closer arm flexed. The door shook in place.

No visible hand touched it.

Diane crossed the blue line.

She caught the outer edge before the door could swing farther and held it away from the group. Pressure pushed against the slab from the corridor side, then eased. The closer drew the door toward the frame with ordinary resistance.

“Everyone against the north wall,” she said.

Evan kept filming while he moved. The assistant pulled Miriam with her. Abby backed into the corridor, maintaining a view of Diane, the door, and the group.

Diane let the closer finish. The latch struck the plate and failed to seat. The door bounced once, then rested open a finger’s width.

The fan resumed its steady push. Diane’s recorder brushed her coat once, then again, loud enough in the quiet to seem separate from her until she felt the vibration against her ribs.

“Time,” she said.

Abby stated it.

Diane pressed the door shut until the latch caught. She tested the lever. The hardware held.

Evan spoke toward his camera. “The door moved immediately after Miriam said the presence was at the threshold.”

“Stop narrating,” Diane said.

He looked at her. “Everyone saw it.”

“We saw the door move. We lack the cause.”

“There was no one near it.”

“The assistant stood closest. Miriam stood within reach before the first impact. Air pressure changed earlier. The closer arm creaked before movement. Abby lacks the lower hinge and floor contact in frame.”

The assistant said, “I was three feet away.”

“I am recording distance, not blame.”

Miriam touched her assistant’s wrist. “No one here caused it intentionally.”

Evan stepped toward the corridor. “Diane, come inside. It wanted you in the room.”

“You called my name into the corridor.”

“After Miriam found it.”

“The assistant supplied it.”

“The door moved after the name.”

“The door moved after several statements, a reflection test, a pressure change, and a hardware sound.”

He raised his voice. “Diane, get in here.”

Evan’s shout echoed down the corridor and returned from the open mechanical spaces, shortened to one hard syllable.

Diane looked at Abby. “Record that call.”

“Marked.”

Miriam said, “Diane, I want to ask one question with your permission.”

Diane faced her. “Ask it without assigning a speaker.”

Miriam nodded. “Does the impression become stronger when Diane enters?”

“That is a question to yourself.”

“Yes.”

Diane stepped fully into the room and stopped on the blue line.

Miriam closed her eyes.

Evan’s camera moved close to her face.

Diane said, “Wider frame.”

He took one step back.

Miriam’s breathing slowed. She turned slightly toward Diane.

“The pressure increases,” she said.

“Where?”

“Behind my left eye and across my throat.”

“Any environmental change?”

Abby checked the thermometer. “No temperature change. Fan steady. Door latched.”

Miriam opened her eyes. “I interpret the pressure as resistance to correction.”

Evan said, “Because Diane keeps challenging it.”

“Because correction is part of what we have been discussing,” Diane said. “That interpretation has a direct information path.”

Miriam nodded. “Yes.”

The latch clicked behind Diane.

Everyone turned.

The door stayed shut.

Diane approached it without touching the lever. The latch bolt remained within the strike. The closer arm sat straight. She looked through the wired glass into the empty corridor.

A low sound came from Abby’s camera speaker.

Abby lowered the camera and checked the monitor.

“What?” Evan asked.

“I heard something through the headphone return.”

“From the hall?”

“I cannot place it.”

Diane said, “No playback in the room.”

Evan stepped closer. “It could answer the name.”

“Or it could be clothing, handling noise, the latch, or speech caught beneath the door movement. Preserve the file.”

Abby removed one earcup. “I heard one syllable.”

“What syllable?” the assistant asked.

Abby looked at Diane.

“Leave it untranscribed,” Diane said.

Evan turned his camera toward Abby. “Play it once.”

“No.”

“We need to know whether it said her name.”

Diane opened the door and pointed into the corridor. “Everyone out of this room. Same order as entry. Abby keeps recording. We review the audio after positions and hardware are documented.”

Evan stayed where he was. “The strongest response of the night may be sitting on her camera.”

“The recording remains on the card.”

“You are delaying it.”

“I am preventing five people from supplying five versions before we hear the source.”

Miriam moved first. She guided her assistant into the corridor. Abby followed backward, holding Diane and the door in frame. Evan left last, still filming the room.

Diane closed the door and tested the latch. This time it seated cleanly.

She placed a strip of tamper tape across the latch edge and wrote the time over the seam.

Evan looked at the tape. “So the room is sealed.”

“The door is marked. No one reenters until facilities checks the closer, hinges, pressure, and floor contact.”

Abby removed the camera’s headphone cable and showed both ends in frame. She locked the source card and inserted a fresh one.

“Original secured,” she said.

They moved to the equipment alcove. Diane set the facilities case on the bench but left the meter and flashlight sealed inside. Abby placed her camera on the towel, connected headphones, and opened the final thirty seconds of the source clip.

“Normal speed,” Diane said. “One play. No proposed words until everyone writes what they heard.”

Evan opened his mouth.

“Write first.”

Abby handed out blank evidence slips. Miriam took one. Her assistant held the pencil without moving.

Abby played the clip.

The door struck the stop. Metal rattled. The assistant gasped. Evan’s shoes scraped concrete. Diane ordered everyone back. Beneath the second rattle, a low sound occupied less than half a second.

It resembled a voice only because its pitch held steady through the surrounding impacts.

The clip ended.

No one spoke.

Diane wrote: LOW VOWEL OR THROAT SOUND — SOURCE UNLOCATED.

She folded the slip before looking up.

